   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 38108222,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 3831378,
    "poster_id": 212018,
    "post_rendered": "For a positive integer <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" >,</span> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/f/8ffd01cdc48042ce7c67d8edcd4904809831cfe4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" > be the coprime positive integers with<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/6459994147c16d9fe8c2b22410466f5eecb51288.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{a_n}{b_n}=1+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\dots+\\frac{1}{n}.\\]\"  width=\"208\" height=\"39\" >Show that the following two assertions are equivalent (i.e., both true or both false):<br>\n(i) There are infinitely many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/f/8ffd01cdc48042ce7c67d8edcd4904809831cfe4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" > is not divisible by <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/8/d08d793e5c8445dff4a9a27052ca7b7619cb4578.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2027$\"  width=\"36\" height=\"12\" >.</span><br>\n(ii) There are infinitely many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/f/8ffd01cdc48042ce7c67d8edcd4904809831cfe4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" > is not divisible by <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/1/76183d9d29fec8e2bf3f6cbe497cdbfce35153c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2027^{2025}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "For a positive integer $n$, let $a_n$ and $b_n$ be the coprime positive integers with\n\\[\\frac{a_n}{b_n}=1+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\dots+\\frac{1}{n}.\\]\nShow that the following two assertions are equivalent (i.e., both true or both false):\n(i) There are infinitely many $n$ for which $b_n$ is not divisible by $2027$.\n(ii) There are infinitely many $n$ for which $b_n$ is not divisible by $2027^{2025}$.",
    "username": "Tintarn",
